le":"Manger"},"Power":{"file":"dictionary/3f161924099fe2f845c8fcf541bba92a038ee5a46d124c21af068a675506c238.json","title":"Power"},"ProverbsBookof":{"file":"dictionary/c4ab4305f0d60bcd8b3ce7865a75d28618ff906dd84677e1c2f4ebbf9e020378.json","title":"Proverbs, Book of"},"WisdomLiterature":{"file":"dictionary/60bfca9559688f5416772ffa946be9b716b746bcc70e530673ed00ad7da5af62.json","title":"Wisdom Literature*"}},"dictionaryRelated":[{"id":"Animals","title":"Animals"},{"id":"Birds","title":"Birds"},{"id":"Harrow","title":"Harrow*"},{"id":"JobBookof","title":"Job, Book of"},{"id":"Liberty","title":"Liberty*"},{"id":"Manger","title":"Manger"},{"id":"Power","title":"Power"},{"id":"ProverbsBookof","title":"Proverbs, Book of"},{"id":"WisdomLiterature","title":"Wisdom Literature*"}],"dictionaryWords":[],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["Understanding Job"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["For as long as people have read Job’s story, they have struggled with the tension between the positive and negative aspects of Job’s character. The book’s approval of Job in the opening prologue is unequivocal (",{"href":"?bref=Job.1.1","r":"Job.1.1","rawR":"Job.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.1.1","value":"Job.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:1"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Job.1.8","r":"Job.1.8","rawR":"Job.1.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.1.8","value":"Job.1.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.2.3","r":"Job.2.3","rawR":"Job.2.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.2.3","value":"Job.2.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:3"},"), yet we later hear God interrogate Job: “Who is this that questions my wisdom with such ignorant words?” (",{"href":"?bref=Job.38.2","r":"Job.38.2","rawR":"Job.38.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.38.2","value":"Job.38.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"38:2"},"). “Will you discredit my justice and condemn me just to prove you are right?” (",{"href":"?bref=Job.40.8","r":"Job.40.8","rawR":"Job.40.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.40.8","value":"Job.40.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"40:8"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["God’s questions have led various interpreters to accuse Job of talking too much and even of confusing God’s work with the work of Satan. Some contemporary interpretations side with Job’s counselors in blaming Job for his suffering. Still others follow Eliphaz (",{"href":"?bref=Job.5.17-27","r":"Job.5.17-27","rawR":"Job.5.17-27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.5.17-27","value":"Job.5.17-27"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:17-27"},") and Elihu (",{"href":"?bref=Job.33.15-33","r":"Job.33.15-33","rawR":"Job.33.15-33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.33.15-33","value":"Job.33.15-33"}],"t":"ref","v":"33:15-33"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.36.7-17","r":"Job.36.7-17","rawR":"Job.36.7-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.36.7-17","value":"Job.36.7-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"36:7-17"},") in arguing that Job’s suffering was God’s loving and sanctifying chastisement. Even the faithful are not exempt from such discipline (",{"href":"?bref=Heb.12.6-12","r":"Heb.12.6-12","rawR":"Heb.12.6-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.12.6-12","value":"Heb.12.6-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 12:6-12"},"; see ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.3.11-12","r":"Pr.3.11-12","rawR":"Pr.3.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.3.11-12","value":"Pr.3.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 3:11-12"},"), but this explanation flies in the face of the rationale given in the book’s opening verses."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Job suffered because God wanted to prove Job’s integrity to Satan. God’s own judgment of Job’s life was positive from start to finish (",{"href":"?bref=Job.1.1","r":"Job.1.1","rawR":"Job.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.1.1","value":"Job.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 1:1"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Job.1.8","r":"Job.1.8","rawR":"Job.1.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.1.8","value":"Job.1.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.2.3","r":"Job.2.3","rawR":"Job.2.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.2.3","value":"Job.2.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.42.7","r":"Job.42.7","rawR":"Job.42.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.42.7","value":"Job.42.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"42:7"},"). This perspective carries through to the New Testament, where Job is an example of endurance that we should imitate (",{"href":"?bref=Jas.5.11","r":"Jas.5.11","rawR":"Jas.5.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jas.5.11","value":"Jas.5.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jas 5:11"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Job’s reactions are normal. He does not stand like a rock in reverent stoicism as though he were unaffected by his trials. Neither does he trumpet heroic defiance of his troubles, as though they could never get him down. He reacts in a natural human way. Job rages, protests, moans, and vacillates between confidence and despair, but he never gives up. He does not curse God, and he does not make a false confession of guilt in the hope that God will then let up on him. Although Job cannot understand his situation, he knows that his answer will be found in God (",{"href":"?bref=Job.19.25-27","r":"Job.19.25-27","rawR":"Job.19.25-27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.19.25-27","value":"Job.19.25-27"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 19:25-27"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.1.1","r":"Job.1.1","rawR":"Job.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.1.1","value":"Job.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 1:1"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Job.1.8","r":"Job.1.8","rawR":"Job.1.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.1.8","value":"Job.1.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.2.3","r":"Job.2.3","rawR":"Job.2.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.2.3","value":"Job.2.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.5.17-18","r":"Job.5.17-18","rawR":"Job.5.17-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.5.17-18","value":"Job.5.17-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:17-18"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.19.25-27","r":"Job.19.25-27","rawR":"Job.19.25-27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.19.25-27","value":"Job.19.25-27"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:25-27"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.33.15-28","r":"Job.33.15-28","rawR":"Job.33.15-28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.33.15-28","value":"Job.33.15-28"}],"t":"ref","v":"33:15-28"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.36.8-15","r":"Job.36.8-15","rawR":"Job.36.8-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.36.8-15","value":"Job.36.8-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"36:8-15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.38.2","r":"Job.38.2","rawR":"Job.38.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.38.2","value":"Job.38.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"38:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.40.8","r":"Job.40.8","rawR":"Job.40.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.40.8","value":"Job.40.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"40:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.42.7","r":"Job.42.7","rawR":"Job.42.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.42.7","value":"Job.42.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"42:7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.3.11","r":"Pr.3.11","rawR":"Pr.3.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.3.11","value":"Pr.3.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 3:11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.12.7","r":"Heb.12.7","rawR":"Heb.12.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.12.7","value":"Heb.12.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 12:7"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.12.10","r":"Heb.12.10","rawR":"Heb.12.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.12.10","value":"Heb.12.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"10"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jas.5.11","r":"Jas.5.11","rawR":"Jas.5.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jas.5.11","value":"Jas.5.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jas 5:11"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:UnderstandingJob","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":42,"endVerse":17,"startChapter":38,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"UnderstandingJob","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[38,39,40,41,42],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5ca39c43e11da8de13a2a430f8b5cd8f60e873839923ba2070e996921f8e26bd","sourceItemName":"UnderstandingJob","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":106,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.38.1-42.17","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"Understanding Job","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Understanding Job","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.38.1-40.5","r":"Job.38.1-40.5","rawR":"Job.38.1-40.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.38.1-40.5","value":"Job.38.1-40.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"38:1–40:5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," God challenges Job, who acknowledges his inability to judge the moral world by demonstrating his ignorance of the natural world’s cosmic (",{"href":"?bref=Job.38.4-21","r":"Job.38.4-21","rawR":"Job.38.4-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.38.4-21","value":"Job.38.4-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"38:4-21"},") and meteorological elements (",{"href":"?bref=Job.38.22-38","r":"Job.38.22-38","rawR":"Job.38.22-38","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.38.22-38","value":"Job.38.22-38"}],"t":"ref","v":"38:22-38"},"), animals, and birds (",{"href":"?bref=Job.38.39-39.30","r":"Job.38.39-39.30","rawR":"Job.38.39-39.30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.38.39-39.30","value":"Job.38.39-39.30"}],"t":"ref","v":"38:39–39:30"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.38.1-40.5","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":40,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":38,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Job.38.1-40.5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[38,39,40],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"a22ac306c0f72f17aefd51662864c5054e1721c24aaee43c4890ef2b6b7a5977","sourceItemName":"Job.38.1-40.5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5919,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.38.1-40.5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Job.38.1-40.5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.39.5-7","r":"Job.39.5-7","rawR":"Job.39.5-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.5-7","value":"Job.39.5-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:5-7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["wild donkey . . . hates"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["scorns"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},") ",{"c":["the noise of the city:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This is the first in a series of animals that scorn others who are their inferiors in some way (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Job.39.18","r":"Job.39.18","rawR":"Job.39.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.18","value":"Job.39.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:18"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Job.39.22","r":"Job.39.22","rawR":"Job.39.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.22","value":"Job.39.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.41.29","r":"Job.41.29","rawR":"Job.41.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.41.29","value":"Job.41.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"41:29"},"). These images illustrate how God scorns the opposition of a man like Job (see ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.2.4","r":"Ps.2.4","rawR":"Ps.2.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.2.4","value":"Ps.2.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 2:4"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.39.5-7","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":39,"endVerse":7,"startChapter":39,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Job.39.5-7","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[39],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c8413243b94b09900d4579566ee33e1ee561fc1617ec7c62f02f9344c3eba5a7","sourceItemName":"Job.39.5-7","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5929,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.39.5-7","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Job.39.5-7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.39.9-12","r":"Job.39.9-12","rawR":"Job.39.9-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.9-12","value":"Job.39.9-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:9-12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Now extinct and already rare by Moses’ time, the ",{"c":["wild ox"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was the most powerful land animal in early Canaan. This Old Testament symbol of strength (",{"href":"?bref=Num.23.22","r":"Num.23.22","rawR":"Num.23.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.23.22","value":"Num.23.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 23:22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.24.8","r":"Num.24.8","rawR":"Num.24.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.24.8","value":"Num.24.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.33.17","r":"Deut.33.17","rawR":"Deut.33.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.33.17","value":"Deut.33.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 33:17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.29.6","r":"Ps.29.6","rawR":"Ps.29.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.29.6","value":"Ps.29.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 29:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.92.10","r":"Ps.92.10","rawR":"Ps.92.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.92.10","value":"Ps.92.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"92:10"},") was never ",{"c":["tamed"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.1.28","r":"Gen.1.28","rawR":"Gen.1.28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.1.28","value":"Gen.1.28"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 1:28"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.9.2","r":"Gen.9.2","rawR":"Gen.9.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.9.2","value":"Gen.9.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.8.5-6","r":"Ps.8.5-6","rawR":"Ps.8.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.8.5-6","value":"Ps.8.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 8:5-6"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.39.9-12","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":39,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":39,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Job.39.9-12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[39],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8fe26a18d9463e79bca442156e0e29a98d535879e5f499cc53636a3b4bf62894","sourceItemName":"Job.39.9-12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5930,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.39.9-12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Job.39.9-12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.39.13-18","r":"Job.39.13-18","rawR":"Job.39.13-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.13-18","value":"Job.39.13-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:13-18"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," In the ancient Near East, the ",{"c":["ostrich"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," had a reputation as a bird that God had ",{"c":["deprived"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," of ",{"c":["wisdom."],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.39.13-18","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":39,"endVerse":18,"startChapter":39,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Job.39.13-18","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[39],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"2f27a61c37058b7cd3192c1a42ab7ba289f0dd8c563b92e758de4cabec624ef3","sourceItemName":"Job.39.13-18","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5931,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.39.13-18","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Job.39.13-18","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.39.14-16","r":"Job.39.14-16","rawR":"Job.39.14-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.14-16","value":"Job.39.14-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:14-16"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ostrich is a symbol of neglect for her ",{"c":["young"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=Lam.4.3","r":"Lam.4.3","rawR":"Lam.4.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lam.4.3","value":"Lam.4.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lam 4:3"},") because she (1) ",{"c":["lays her eggs on top of the earth"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[";"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (2) appears to leave her eggs to ",{"c":["die"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," when a predator approaches them (although she is probably attempting to lure the predator away from the nest); and (3) lays her eggs with several other hens in one nest, so most of the eggs are ",{"c":["not her own"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.39.14-16","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":39,"endVerse":16,"startChapter":39,"startVerse":14}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Job.39.14-16","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[39],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0383c635d1cff22c128915e4755af79427dfcd3321c724e215395a3926ccf59c","sourceItemName":"Job.39.14-16","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5932,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.39.14-16","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Job.39.14-16","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.39.18","r":"Job.39.18","rawR":"Job.39.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.18","value":"Job.39.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:18"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["passes"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["scorns"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},")",{"c":[" the swiftest horse with its rider:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," See ",{"href":"?bref=Job.39.7","r":"Job.39.7","rawR":"Job.39.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.7","value":"Job.39.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:7"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Job.39.22","r":"Job.39.22","rawR":"Job.39.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.22","value":"Job.39.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.41.29","r":"Job.41.29","rawR":"Job.41.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.41.29","value":"Job.41.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"41:29"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.39.18","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":39,"endVerse":18,"startChapter":39,"startVerse":18}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Job.39.18","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[39],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3e2afd5f59f6e4dc01f6b9ac142a49f5ca5972b875415523a3440df0038740e9","sourceItemName":"Job.39.18","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5933,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.39.18","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Job.39.18","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.39.24","r":"Job.39.24","rawR":"Job.39.24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.24","value":"Job.39.24"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:24"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["ram’s horn"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was sounded in combat to call for the charge (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.6.4-6","r":"Josh.6.4-6","rawR":"Josh.6.4-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.6.4-6","value":"Josh.6.4-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 6:4-6"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.39.24","refs":[{"book":"JOB","endChapter":39,"endVerse":24,"startChapter":39,"startVerse":24}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Job.39.24","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JOB","sourceChapterCoverage":[39],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e1fde14a4ad0250a4cca78be9857afe8b060cded5c9b198b93ffca391f004315","sourceItemName":"Job.39.24","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5934,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JOB","canonicalOSIS":"Job","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Job"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Job.39.24","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Job.39.24","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Job.39.30","r":"Job.39.30","rawR":"Job.39.30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.39.30","value":"Job.39.30"}],"t":"ref","v":"39:30"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Where there’s a carcass, there you’ll find it"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"—feeding on the remains (",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.39.17-19","r":"Ezek.39.17-19","rawR":"Ezek.39.17-19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.39.17-19","value":"Ezek.39.17-19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezek 39:17-19"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.24.28","r":"Matt.24.28","rawR":"Matt.24.28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.24.28","value":"Matt.24.28"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 24:28"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Luke.17.37","r":"Luke.17.37","rawR":"Luke.17.37","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.17.37","value":"Luke.17.37"}],"t":"ref","v":"Luke 17:37"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Job.39.30","refs":[{"book